{"base":"1469e0c33e6eea7e1b3c4a2ff605fcaaf997ba6b","description":"Previously, we reused the radicle key to create the iroh endpoint, which is also based on an ed25519 keypair. This allowed to keep the design minimal, and avoid having to manage another key. \n\nHowever, there are a couple of problems with this approach:\n- radicle uses ssh-agent for signing, which avoids loading the private key into memory in radicle. We broke this contract by prompting the user for passphrase to decrypt the radicle private key, which would then be loaded into memory\n- It became clear that you may want to have a single radicle identity have more than one endpoint, e.g. for serving from both radicle-desktop and from the cli on an always on server. By decoupling the two keys, a single user can more naturally have more than one endpoint for serving content.  \n- radicle aims to decouple the user did from the nodeID in the mid-term. By using a separate key we future proof the radicle-artifact implementation \n- it's generally good practice to use separate keys where posssible to reduce \"blast radius\" from a key leaking or getting compromised. \n\n\nGiven all this, this patch introduces a new ed25519 key that is stored in the radicle home directory and is used to create the iroh endpoint. One consequence of this change is that we now store the full endpoint id in `iroh://` location URIs when running the serve command. For backwards compatibility with older versions of the cli, bare `iroh://` uris will be interpreted using the old logic, deriving the endpoint ID from the author DID.","oid":"f7f8dde153daedb728352bfd37d04e307531a3bd","type":"revision"}